TASK
Create sizing guidance for {{product_and_variant}}.
Exact supplier chart: {{supplier_size_chart}}
Notes on units/body versus garment measurements: {{measurement_notes}}
Relevant review sample: {{review_fit_sample}}
Measured sample results, if available: {{sample_measurements}}

First determine whether the chart measures the wearer's body or the garment. If that is unclear, flag the ambiguity and do not convert it into a fit recommendation. Preserve every source value, unit, range and stated tolerance. If converting centimeters and inches, use 1 inch = 2.54 cm and show appropriate rounding without inventing precision.

Explain how to compare the relevant measurements for this garment, such as bust, waist, hip, length, rise or inseam. Do not invent missing values or assume the same chart applies to other products from the store. Do not equate S/M/L or numerical labels with a universal regional size. Avoid a blanket instruction to 'size up' or 'size down'.

Summarize review observations only with their sample size, selected variants and limitations. If feedback contradicts the chart or sample, explain the conflict and mark the item for review. Do not extrapolate from one person's measurements to every shopper. Avoid body judgments and unnecessary personal details.

Return a clean chart, concise customer-facing instructions, a neutral fit note and internal questions for the supplier. Distinguish a known fit range from a subjective fit preference. If essential data is absent, return the exact missing fields instead of a speculative chart.

